On Tue, 10 Feb 2004 10:53:50 -0800, Phil Yellott wrote:

> I have a P200 Laptop running Win98 , it has 2 Gig Hard drive and 24 Megs of Ram.
> I am debating what OS to install:  98SE, Linux, Dos 6.22?

First of all, there's no use installing DOS, IMHO.

I tried Win98 (first edition) on my laptop, but it didn't work too
well. Now I run W2K and it's somewhat stable, though it most probably
won't run on a computer with your specs (mine's a 366 MHz with 96 MB RAM,
and it's still rather slow for me). Also, when idling, about 50 MB of RAM
is consumed just by the system alone. Overall, I wouldn't recommend
Windows - it's too slow and unstable for my tastes.

Also, on Windows you will have to rely on pre-compiled binaries. Of
course, you can compile stuff yourself if you have the required tools and
libraries, but again, your specs somewhat limits that (though you *could*
compile them on another computer and just copy them to the laptop, I dunno).

Or if you don't need/want to compile in special options or patches, you
could just skip the above paragraph alltogether.

OTOH, there are quite a few roguelikes that are not opensource and where
the developers only supply binaries for Windows systems, or rely on
Windows code and cannot be ported. Most mainstream RLs should run on
anything, though.

I'd go for Linux (the only reason I run W2K on mine is that I need a
Windows platform for various reference purposes). If you are already
comfortable with it and don't care if you miss out on some potentially
good Windows-only roguelikes, I see no reason not to.

Also, use textmode. Navigating the mouse pointer with my touchpad is
probably one of the most frustrating experiences of my life. Or, if you
*must* run X, at least use a WM with sloppy focus (fvwm and blackbox are
my personal favourites).

